    "case_id": "501",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her mid-60s presented to the emergency department with sudden, dull left flank pain, rated 8 of 10, radiating to the lateral abdomen and accompanied by nausea. No exacerbating or alleviating factors were noted. She was a nonsmoker and did not report recreational drugs or alcohol. Family history notable for father with renal cell carcinoma. Medical history included hypertension, basal cell carcinoma in remission, and an autoimmune condition diagnosed 16 years ago. Patient had been on long-term azathioprine therapy (125 mg daily, later reduced to 100 mg daily) and sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim. Four years after initial diagnosis, she experienced unintentional weight loss, fever, polyarthralgia, and fatigue.\n\nPatient was hemodynamically stable. Physical examination revealed tenderness at the left costovertebral angle.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Hemoglobin: 114 g/L (Normal: 125-165)    * WBC: 6.17 ×10⁹/L (Normal: 4-10.8)    * ANC: 2.75 ×10⁹/L (Normal: 1.5-7.8)    * ALC: 3.32 ×10⁹/L (Normal: 0.85-3.9)    * AMC: 1.15 ×10⁹/L (Normal: 0.299-0.95)    * AEC: 0.12 ×10⁹/L (Normal: 0.02-0.5)    * LDH: 3.03 µkat/L (Normal: 2.33-4.67)    * Creatinine: 95.47 µmol/L (Normal: 53.04-97.24)    * ESR: 54 mm/hr (Normal: 0-20)    * CRP: 6.1 mg/L (Normal: <8)  - Imaging Studies:    * CT Scan Title: Contrast-enhanced computed tomography of the abdomen    * CT Scan Description: 7 cm × 5 cm × 5 cm heterogenous left adrenal mass containing regions of necrosis, with local extension into and invasion of the pancreatic tail, left hemidiaphragm, and spleen, along with the presence of multiple enlarged regional lymph nodes.",
